{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and released in 2010, isn't just a movie about facebook; it's a meticulously crafted character study of ambition, betrayal, and the complex cost of innovation. it's a sharp, intelligent, and often painfully realistic portrayal of mark zuckerberg's rise to power and the tumultuous beginnings of what would become one of the world’s most influential platforms.\n\nthe film’s narrative structure is clever and fragmented, mirroring the disjointed and often unreliable perspectives of those involved. we’re presented with multiple interwoven storylines, primarily focusing on mark zuckerberg (played with chilling intensity by richard maltz), christopher casilla (jared leto, delivering a performance of breathtaking vulnerability and arrogance), and elizabeth berger (a captivating and nuanced anne hendon). fincher masterfully uses flashbacks and present-day scenes to build a compelling and often morally ambiguous case for zuckerberg’s actions.\n\nthe script, penned by armando bojars and david archer, is exceptionally strong. the dialogue is crisp, witty, and often laced with cutting sarcasm. it’s not just clever banter; it's a reflection of the sharp, often cynical, world of tech entrepreneurs. the film excels at portraying the competitive and cutthroat environment in which zuckerberg and his team operated, highlighting the intense pressure and the ruthless pursuit of success.\n\nthe performances are uniformly excellent. maltz’s zuckerberg is a fascinating study of a man grappling with genius, social isolation, and a burgeoning sense of entitlement. leto’s casilla is a heartbreaking portrayal of a brilliant programmer consumed by jealousy and a desperate desire for recognition. hendon’s berger is a strong and independent woman navigating a complex and often manipulative relationship with zuckerberg. the supporting cast, including joe gordon-lewis as greg brady and max galland as eduardo salas, all deliver memorable and believable performances.\n\nthe film’s visual style is understated yet impactful. the cinematography is sleek and modern, reflecting the aesthetic of the facebook platform. the soundtrack, featuring a blend of electronic music and classical pieces, is equally effective in setting the tone and enhancing the emotional impact of the scenes.\n\nthe social network isn’t a simple story of success. it’s a cautionary tale about the unintended consequences of technological innovation and the human cost of ambition. it explores themes of friendship, betrayal, power, and the nature of connection in the digital age. it raises profound questions about the ethics of social media and the impact it has on our society.\n\nwhile the film takes liberties with historical accuracy, it’s ultimately more concerned with exploring the psychological and emotional complexities of the characters involved. it’s a film that stays with you long after the credits roll, prompting reflection on the world we live in and the forces that shape it. it's a must-see for anyone interested in technology, business, or simply compelling storytelling. it’s a brilliant, challenging, and ultimately unforgettable film.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.9215933084487915}, {"token": "▁social", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "▁network", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.11325513571500778}, {"token": "▁directed", "logprob": -0.002525594551116228}, {"token": "▁by", "logprob": -2.4318398573086597e-05}, {"token": "▁david", "logprob": -0.0064103929325938225}, {"token": "▁fin", "logprob": -9.894321920000948e-06}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -5.4834770708112046e-05}, {"token": "▁and", "logprob": -0.11289060860872269}, {"token": "▁released", "logprob": -0.05018049478530884}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -1.78813775